    {
      "chunk": {
        "chunk_id": "runbook-k8s-crash-loops-and-rollouts#diagnosis-crash-loop-or-image-pull-1",
        "doc_path": "fixtures/knowledge-overlays/kubernetes/runbook-k8s-crash-loops-and-rollouts.md",
        "title": "Runbook: Kubernetes rollouts that stall and pods that crash loop",
        "heading": "Diagnosis: crash loop or image pull",
        "text": "1. Call `get_k8s_like_events` for the service. `CrashLoopBackOff` means the code starts and\n   exits; `BackOff` after an `OOMKilled` termination means memory (see the readiness and\n   memory runbook); `ImagePullBackOff` means the image itself.\n2. For a crash loop, read `get_service_logs`: the last lines before each exit name the\n   error. An exception tagged with a release, such as `[build <release>]` or\n   `(version=<release>)`, points at the code of that release.\n3. For an image-pull back-off the logs are empty. Compare the newest revision with the one\n   before it in `get_recent_deployments`.\n4. Compare times with `get_recent_deployments` and the `error_rate` metric. A crash loop\n   that begins within minutes of the service's own release makes that release a suspect. A\n   crash loop on a service that was not released points elsewhere: memory, configuration, or\n   a dependency it needs at start-up.",
        "checksum": "140aaf4db2f6",
        "provenance": {
          "source": "fixtures/knowledge-overlays/kubernetes",
          "kind": "synthetic runbook authored for RECAP V1",
